//? Title: Built-in datasets and the local download cache
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

Use `dm::data("iris")` to load a fresh table by name. The same lookup covers
the small tables embedded in the library and larger tabular datasets that
are downloaded on demand. `dm::dataset_names()` returns the canonical names as a
`dm::Vector<std::string>` in lexical order. `dm::datasets::catalog()` adds
dimensions, storage modes and approximate download sizes.
Existing calls such as `dm::data::iris()` still work. C++ code that imports the
legacy dataset namespace can use `datamunge::data`.

> Load familiar teaching data

The classic collection includes experimental designs, repeated measurements,
count and categorical examples, regression benchmarks, and historical time
series. Source spelling such as `ToothGrowth`, `CodParasites` and
`CrabSatellites` works alongside the canonical names. Exact spelling matters
for the two pairs of distinct data sources: `CO2` is plant uptake and `co2` is
atmospheric CO2, while `Titanic` is R's contingency table and `titanic` is the
passenger-level table.

Numerically coded factors in the added snapshots keep their labels as strings.
Missing cells remain null. Contingency arrays have one row per combination
of levels and a count column. Time series include the original observation
time. The `cod_parasites` table includes counts, prevalence and the sampling
covariates from the original countreg data. The `crab_satellites` table
records color and spine condition, carapace width in cm, weight in kg and
the satellite count.

```{cpp, id=named-loaders, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/data.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    std::println("| Lookup | Rows | Columns |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const auto* name : {"iris", "penguins", "ToothGrowth", "CodParasites", "CrabSatellites"}) {
        const auto table = dm::data(name);
        std::println("| `{}` | {} | {} |", name, table.nrows(), table.ncols());
    }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Lookup | Rows | Columns |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| `iris` | 150 | 5 |
| `penguins` | 344 | 8 |
| `ToothGrowth` | 60 | 3 |
| `CodParasites` | 1254 | 10 |
| `CrabSatellites` | 173 | 5 |
// result_end

> Storage and discovery

Embedded tables work offline. Each added CSV is at most 64 KiB, and the
additions together use at most 256 KiB of CSV payload before compilation.
Large tabular data such as diamonds and California housing are fetched on the
first lookup and reused from the verified cache. Set `DATAMUNGE_DATA_DIR` to
choose the cache root and `DATAMUNGE_DATA_OFFLINE=1` to disable downloads.
MNIST, Fashion-MNIST, CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100 use
`dm::datasets::ImageLoader("mnist", "train")` and its corresponding dataset
names to fetch and read batches. The Karate Club graph has a typed loader.

```{cpp, id=registered-datasets, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/data.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    const dm::Vector<std::string> names = dm::dataset_names();
    std::println("Registered datasets — {}.\n", names.size());
    const auto catalogue = dm::datasets::catalog();
    std::print("{}", catalogue.to_md());
}
```
